---
// A board that checked something the engine claimed, and the table it reported.
//
// The page is the record of one run: a still of the part, what the pins said, and the cases the run does not cover. It
// quotes the run rather than repeating it, because the build has no board attached.
import type { Artifact } from '../data/artifacts';

interface Props {
  artifact: Artifact;
}

const { artifact } = Astro.props;
---

<p class="run__lede">{artifact.summary}</p>

<figure class="run__figure device__figure">
  <img src="/board-ecp5.jpg" alt="The iCESugar-Pro v1.3 module seated in its carrier board, lit and connected." />
  <figcaption>
    The part the run was made on: an ECP5 LFE5U-25F on an iCESugar-Pro v1.3 module. The on-board iCELink carries three
    ECP5 balls to the host as GPIOs, so the verdict is read over the programming cable and no external wiring is
    involved. The carrier's two USB-C ports reach no FPGA.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What the board reported</h2>
  <p>
    A volume of 11,172 records was written by the engine. The first 512 of them were read back off the medium, which is
    10,240 coordinate values, and supplied to the part as a ROM. The part decoded each value and accumulated the three
    axes; the host computed the same sum by division while the die computes it with a reciprocal multiply.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Coordinate values checked</td>
        <td>10,240, from 512 records</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Host sum, by division</td>
        <td><code>00002BFC</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Die sum, by reciprocal multiply</td>
        <td><code>00002BFC</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Values outside the 11,172-coordinate alphabet</td>
        <td>0</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>One value changed to 11,172, the first value outside it</td>
        <td>Sum mismatch, one value refused</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Block RAM</td>
        <td>9 of 56 blocks, 16 percent</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Logic</td>
        <td>219 flip-flops, 448 combinational</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Timing at a 40 MHz constraint</td>
        <td>76.99 MHz</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The last row is not a claim about speed. It says the image was built at a rate the part holds. Where the decode
    stops matching is measured by the separate image that sweeps the valid range, which is built at 100, 120, 125 and
    140 MHz.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What the run establishes</h2>
  <p>
    The addresses on the medium are coordinates, and a circuit that did not compute them says so. There is no table in
    the path on either side: the host reads the keys off the medium, and the part recomputes each coordinate from its
    value. A hash table cannot make this statement, because the statement is that no table exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    The negative control is what makes the pass worth reading. One value at index 5,000 was changed to 11,172, and both
    the sum and the refusal count responded. An indicator that can only show one value proves nothing.
  </p>
  <pre class="sr-only">--- values: the coordinate values of 512 records ---
records 512, values 10240, invalid 0, checksum 00002BFC
--- place and route: the volume image at 40 MHz ---
bitstream: out/tagma_volume_verify_top_40mhz.bit
--- program and read the verdict ---
pin_match   = 1
pin_invalid = 0
PASS: the die reproduced 00002BFC over the volume's values</pre>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What the run does not cover</h2>
  <p>
    512 of the 11,172 records are checked, because a ROM holding all 223,440 values does not fit the part. The ROM is
    built by the flow from the host's values, so what is established is the fit between the medium and the circuit, not
    a decoder reading a live medium.
  </p>
  <p>
    No rate is claimed for the engine. The image reads one value per clock and reports a verdict, and the software
    reference is faster on both latency and a full sweep, as the measurements page records.
  </p>
</section>

<dl class="run__meta">
  <div>
    <dt>Check</dt>
    <dd><code>{artifact.source}</code>, in the engine checkout</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Image</dt>
    <dd><code>DESIGN=volume</code>, syntagma hardware flow</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Recorded</dt>
    <dd>2026-11-13</dd>
  </div>
</dl>
